---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

/* Mirrors munchview's store/privacy-policy.md, which is the source of truth
   and the text the Play listing is checked against. Change it there first.

   The two HAD drifted, and it was found on 2026-08-16 while preparing the
   YouTube compliance audit: this page carried the web sign-in section and the
   YouTube API Services disclosure while the markdown carried neither, and
   neither carried the address-and-password sign-in that had already shipped.
   A policy that describes a sign-in the product no longer has, and omits one
   it does, is the failure the studio's legal floor is written to prevent.
   Both were corrected together. Keep them together. */
const updated = 'August 16, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Munchview Privacy Policy — DeftDay Studio"
  description="What Munchview stores on your phone, what it sends, what signing in with Google adds, and how to delete it. No analytics, no advertising SDKs, no tracking."
>
  <SiteChrome />
  <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="s1"></span><span class="s2"></span><span class="s3"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <main id="main" class="legal">
    <span class="eyebrow">Munchview</span>
    <h1>Your taste stays<br /><span class="serif">on your phone.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This policy explains exactly what leaves your device and what does not.
      Munchview has no analytics, no advertising SDKs and no tracking of any
      kind.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="sends">
      <h2 id="sends">What Munchview sends</h2>
      <p>
        Signing in is optional; without it there is no account and nothing about
        you is stored on any server. The app does use one small server of our
        own — a content service that returns the video lists it shows you.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you open the app it asks that service for videos. The request
        carries only the category names or keyword tags you picked and the
        length you chose. To show the YouTube categories that exist where you
        are, the service reads the country of the incoming request at
        Cloudflare's edge — country only, nothing finer, never stored against
        you, and Pro users can override it by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Like any internet request, our hosting provider sees the IP address it
        comes from and keeps short-lived operational logs, a few days, for
        reliability and abuse prevention. We do not use that to identify you, we
        do not build a profile, and we do not sell or share it. The service
        content cache is keyed by category or keyword, never by person. Two
        things on the service are not that cache, and both are named here
        rather than hidden behind an absolute: an abuse counter that holds the
        request's IP address for 120 seconds; the sync record described below,
        stored under your account and only if you sign in; and, if you choose
        to sign in with an address and a password rather than with Google,
        that address and a one-way hash of the password.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="updates">
      <h2 id="updates">Checking for a new version</h2>
      <p>
        There is a second request the app makes on its own, and it is the only
        other one. Every so often Munchview asks
        <a href="https://updates.deftday.com/store-meta.json" rel="noopener"
          >updates.deftday.com</a
        >
        for a small file that says which version is the current one. If that
        number is newer than the one you have, the app tells you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing about you or your phone goes with that request. It has no
        query, no body and no identifier we attach, so the file cannot be
        served differently to you than to anyone else — the server has no way
        of telling one installation from another. As with the content service
        above, the IP address reaches our hosting provider's edge, because
        every request on the internet carries one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The notice itself is made on your phone. Munchview holds no push token
        and is not registered to receive anything, so nobody — including us —
        can send a notification to your device; the app compares two version
        numbers locally and raises its own. You can turn the whole thing off in
        <strong>App settings → Update notifications</strong>, and the check
        stops with it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="signin">
      <h2 id="signin">If you sign in with Google</h2>
      <p>
        Signing in exists for one reason: carrying what one phone knows to
        another. It is entirely optional and the app works fully without it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          We ask Google only who you are — your Google account id, name, email
          address and profile picture. Signing in does <b>not</b> ask for access
          to your YouTube account, your videos, your subscriptions or anything
          else. Connecting YouTube is a separate, optional choice, described in
          its own section below.
          The picture is shown to you in the app and loaded straight from
          Google, the same way any web page would load it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Once signed in, the list below is also stored by our sync service
          under your Google account id, so another phone signed in to the same
          account can pick it up.
        </li>
        <li>
          Our service checks with Google's public keys that a request really
          comes from you before it will store or return anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Sign out</b> stops the syncing and removes the account from the
          phone; the phone keeps its own copy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>We do not sell or share any of it, and we never use it to advertise.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="weblink">
      <h2 id="weblink">Signing in on munchview.app</h2>
      <p>
        There are two ways in, and they end at the same record: Google, or an
        address and a password. The website signs you in itself, separately
        from the app — nothing is typed from one to the other and no code
        changes hands.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>If you use an address and a password</b>, we keep the address and a
        one-way hash of the password. We never store the password itself and
        cannot read it: it goes through 100,000 rounds of PBKDF2-SHA256 with a
        salt of its own and a secret that exists only on our server, so the
        stored value is of no use to anyone who obtains it without that secret.
        The address is kept for one reason — to know which record is yours, and
        to stop one address becoming two accounts. We do not email you and
        there is no mailing list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Signing in with Google on an address that already has a password
        account takes you to that same record rather than making a second one.
        The reverse is refused: a password cannot be set on an address that is
        already an account, because knowing somebody's address would otherwise
        be enough to walk into it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Google hands the browser a short-lived credential. Our service checks
          it once against Google's public keys and gives the browser a session
          of its own in return, so you are not signed out an hour later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>No session is stored.</b> The session is signed rather than
          written down, so there is no list of sessions anywhere to be lost or
          read — whichever way you signed in.
        </li>
        <li>
          The browser then reads the same record described above, under the same
          Google account id. There is one record and two ways of reading it;
          nothing is copied and no second copy exists.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Sign out</b> in the browser clears its session. <b>Delete my
          account data</b> erases the record itself, for both — and, for a
          password account, the address and the hash with it, so the address is
          free to register again.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="device">
      <h2 id="device">What stays on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The categories and keyword tags you picked</li>
        <li>Your last chosen length and the autoplay setting</li>
        <li>Likes, skips, favourites, profiles, and a short recently-watched list</li>
        <li>The playback position of your last video, for "Continue watching"</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you are not signed in, none of this is ever uploaded. "Clear watch
        history" in You, or uninstalling, removes it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="youtube">
      <h2 id="youtube-account">If you connect a platform account</h2>
      <p>
        This is separate from signing in, and entirely optional. Signing in
        never asks for it. Besides YouTube, you can connect Twitch, Vimeo or
        Dailymotion; the same rules apply to each — read-only, your own
        permission, your library read straight from the platform by your
        phone, shown as its own list, deleted by disconnecting. One technical
        difference: for Twitch, Vimeo and Dailymotion the single-use login
        code is turned into your token by our content service, because that
        step needs a key that must not live inside an app — the token comes
        straight back to your phone and is not stored, logged or used there.
        What comes in also differs honestly by platform: likes on Vimeo and
        Dailymotion, and the latest recordings of channels you follow on
        Twitch, which has no likes to read. Specifically for YouTube:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          If you tap <b>Connect YouTube</b>, Google asks you to allow one
          read-only permission (<code>youtube.readonly</code>). We use it for
          one thing: reading the videos you have liked on YouTube, so the app
          can show them as their own list. Connecting also signs you in, because
          the same consent screen covers both — the app tells you so before you
          tap it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The request goes straight from your phone to YouTube</b>, using
          your own permission. Your library does not pass through our servers
          and we never see it. The permission token is not saved; it lasts for
          that one import.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Those likes stay a separate list.</b> Munchview's recommendations
          are built from what you like <i>in Munchview</i>. Your YouTube likes
          are shown beside them, never mixed into them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>We never write anything to your YouTube account.</b> The permission
          we ask for cannot: it is read-only. We do not like, dislike,
          subscribe, comment or change anything there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>We cannot read your YouTube watch history</b>, and we do not try.
          YouTube's API stopped providing it, so no app can. The viewing history
          Munchview shows is the one it recorded itself, on your phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Disconnect</b>, in the app's settings, removes the imported list
          from your phone. You can also withdraw Munchview's access at any time
          from your Google account's permissions page.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="youtube">YouTube playback and thumbnails</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Munchview uses YouTube API Services.</b> That is how it finds videos
        worth your time: our service asks YouTube's API which videos exist for
        a category, a country and a length. It asks about videos, never about
        you — the request carries no identifier of yours, and nothing comes
        back that is about you either.
      </p>
      <p>
        Videos play inside the official YouTube embedded player, and thumbnails
        load from YouTube's image servers. In both cases your device talks to
        YouTube and Google directly, exactly as a browser would, and that
        traffic is governed by their terms and privacy policy, not by ours:
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/t/terms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">YouTube Terms of Service</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The player page itself is served from inside the app, so no third party
        sees your request. Munchview adds no tracking on top of YouTube's player
        and receives nothing back about what you watch.
      </p>

      <h2 id="other-platforms">Videos from other platforms</h2>
      <p>
        Not everything in the feed comes from YouTube. Cards can come from
        Dailymotion, Vimeo, Twitch, PeerTube or the Internet Archive, and each
        plays inside the app — the card says which platform before you tap it.
        Their thumbnails load from their image servers the same way YouTube's
        do.
      </p>
      <p>
        Searching is done by our content service, not by your phone, so those
        platforms never see a search of yours. What they do see is the request
        for a thumbnail or a player, exactly as a browser would send it — no
        account, no identifier and no history goes with it. PeerTube is many
        independently run sites rather than one company, so the request goes to
        the site that hosts that video and its own policy applies; Internet
        Archive videos load as a plain file from archive.org. Once a video is
        playing, the platform's own terms and privacy policy apply:
        <a href="https://www.dailymotion.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dailymotion</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://vimeo.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vimeo</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://www.twitch.tv/p/legal/privacy-notice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twitch</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://archive.org/about/terms.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Internet Archive</a>
        · the hosting PeerTube site's own policy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="children">
      <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
      <p>
        Munchview is not aimed at children and asks for no personal details from
        anyone. Used as a guest — which is how it works unless you choose
        otherwise — it asks for nothing about you and creates no
        account. What still reaches the service is small and named: the country
        the edge sees, the 120-second abuse counter, an error line if something
        breaks, and the text you type into the tag box, which the service
        forwards to a search-suggestion endpoint on your behalf. Signing in
        requires a Google
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="delete">
      <h2 id="delete">Deleting what we hold</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Delete my account data</b>, in You → Your account, erases everything
        stored for your account, at once. If the app is no longer installed, ask
        us and we will do it within 30 days.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="cta" href="/munchview/delete-account">How to delete your account<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="changes">
      <h2 id="changes">Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated version is posted at this same
        address before the change ships.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions: <a href="mailto:privacy@deftday.com">privacy@deftday.com</a>.
        Every DeftDay app is covered on the
        <a href="/privacy">studio privacy page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Türkiye:</b> the disclosure notice required by KVKK art. 10 — the
        same processing, set out in Turkish under that law — is at
        <a href="/kvkk" lang="tr" hreflang="tr">deftday.com/kvkk</a>
        (<span lang="tr">Munchview KVKK Aydınlatma Metni</span>).
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .legal {
    max-width: 78ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 160px var(--gutter) 40px;
  }
  .legal h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.4vw, 1.15rem);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .updated {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .legal-block {
    margin-top: 56px;
    padding-top: 34px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .legal-block h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .legal-block p {
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .legal-block b {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  ul {
    color: var(--muted);
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
